\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The simple type-D operators generate the positive nilradical}

\p{Let #{V=W\oplus W'} be a complex nondegenerate quadratic space with a
chosen split polarization of rank #{\ell\geq 4}. Choose polar-dual bases
#{e_i\in W} and #{e_i'\in W'} with
#{B_Q(e_i,e_j')=\delta_{ij}}, and put #{S=\bigwedge W}. Write #{c_i} for
left creation by #{e_i}, #{a_i} for contraction by #{e_i'},
#{N_i=c_i a_i}, and

##{H_i=N_i-\tfrac12.}

Thus #{\epsilon_i(H_j)=\delta_{ij}} fixes the Cartan coordinates, and the
occupation signs are those of \ref{fcap-0025}. We choose

##{\Phi^+=\{\epsilon_i-\epsilon_j,\ \epsilon_i+\epsilon_j:i<j\}}

and the Borel #{\mathfrak b=\mathfrak h\oplus
\bigoplus_{\alpha\in\Phi^+}\mathfrak g_\alpha}. Equivalently,
#{\mathfrak b} stabilizes the flag
#{\langle e_1\rangle\subset\langle e_1,e_2\rangle\subset\cdots\subset W};
in the ordered Witt basis
#{e_1,\ldots,e_\ell,e_\ell',\ldots,e_1'} it is the upper-triangular Borel.}

\p{For the simple roots
#{\alpha_i=\epsilon_i-\epsilon_{i+1}} along the chain and
#{\alpha_\ell=\epsilon_{\ell-1}+\epsilon_\ell} at the fork, take

##{\begin{aligned}
E_i&=c_i a_{i+1},&F_i&=c_{i+1}a_i
&& (1\leq i<\ell),\\
E_\ell&=c_{\ell-1}c_\ell,&
F_\ell&=a_\ell a_{\ell-1}.&&
\end{aligned}}

The reversed contraction order in #{F_\ell} fixes the bracket sign. On an
increasing exterior word #{u_I}, a chain operator replaces an occupied
#{i+1} by an absent #{i}, or performs the reverse replacement, with
coefficient #{1}; otherwise it gives zero. The fork operators create or
remove the final pair, again with coefficient #{1}, when the corresponding
occupancy condition holds. The CAR then gives

##{[E_i,F_i]=h_i,\qquad
h_i=N_i-N_{i+1}\ (i<\ell),\qquad
h_\ell=N_{\ell-1}+N_\ell-1.}

Under the bivector map these are the products
#{e_i e_{i+1}'}, #{e_{i+1}e_i'}, #{e_{\ell-1}e_\ell}, and
#{e_\ell'e_{\ell-1}'}. Distinct orthogonal factors contribute no hidden
scalar term. TauCeti identifies these exact Clifford representatives with
the numbered type-D matrix roots and coroots
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.typeDQuadraticEquiv_rootGenerator}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.typeDQuadraticEquiv_cartanGenerator}.}

\p{It remains to pass from the simple roots to the chosen Borel. Put

##{X_{ij}=c_i a_j,\qquad Y_{ij}=c_i c_j\qquad(i<j).}

Their Cartan weights are #{\epsilon_i-\epsilon_j} and
#{\epsilon_i+\epsilon_j}. The identities

##{\begin{aligned}
[X_{ij},X_{jk}]&=X_{ik},\\
[X_{i,\ell-1},Y_{\ell-1,\ell}]&=Y_{i,\ell},\\
[X_{j,\ell},Y_{i,\ell}]&=Y_{ij}
\end{aligned}}

for the indicated distinct ordered indices generate every positive
#{X_{ij}} and #{Y_{ij}} from the simple operators. These are all positive
root spaces of #{\mathfrak{so}(V,Q)}, so their span is the full nilradical
#{\mathfrak n^+}. In particular, a vector killed by every #{E_i} is killed
by #{\mathfrak n^+}: brackets and linear combinations preserve its
annihilator. This final root-space argument supplies the Borel bridge; the
formal file presently proves over #{\mathbb Q} annihilation by the Lie span
of the simple positive generators, without identifying that span with a
compatible Borel
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.typeDSpinSerreRepresentation_positiveSimpleRootLieSpan_le_lieAnnihilator}.}
